Chapter 2 — Storage & File Formats

2.1 Row vs Columnar Storage

1. Why does storage format matter?

A Data Engineer's query performance is heavily affected by how data is physically stored.

The basic principle is:

«Less data read = faster queries + lower cost»

For analytical workloads, we frequently query only a few columns from very large datasets.

For example:

SELECT customer_id, revenue
FROM sales;

If the table has 50 columns, we don't need all 50 columns.

A good storage format allows the query engine to avoid reading unnecessary data.

---

2. Row-Oriented Storage

In row-oriented storage, all values belonging to one record are stored together.

Example:

Row 1 → 101, John, Chennai, 25, 50000
Row 2 → 102, Priya, Bangalore, 28, 65000
Row 3 → 103, Arun, Chennai, 30, 80000

Conceptually:

ID | Name  | City      | Age | Salary
---|-------|-----------|-----|-------
101| John  | Chennai   | 25  | 50000
102| Priya | Bangalore | 28  | 65000
103| Arun  | Chennai   | 30  | 80000

When is row storage useful?

Row storage is good when applications frequently need:

- Complete records
- Individual records
- Frequent inserts/updates
- Transactional operations

Example:

SELECT *
FROM customers
WHERE customer_id = 101;

The application wants the complete customer record.

This is common in OLTP systems.

---

3. Column-Oriented Storage

Columnar storage stores values of the same column together.

Instead of:

John, Chennai, 25, 50000
Priya, Bangalore, 28, 65000
Arun, Chennai, 30, 80000

Think:

ID:
101
102
103

Name:
John
Priya
Arun

City:
Chennai
Bangalore
Chennai

Age:
25
28
30

Salary:
50000
65000
80000

This is the basic idea behind formats such as Parquet.

---

4. Why is Columnar Storage Good for Analytics?

Suppose we run:

SELECT salary
FROM employees;

With columnar storage, the engine can primarily read the "salary" column.

It doesn't need to read:

ID
Name
City
Age

This is called:

Column Pruning

«Column pruning = reading only the columns required by the query.»

This reduces:

- Disk I/O
- Network transfer
- Memory usage
- Query execution time
- Cloud storage/compute cost

---

5. Columnar Storage + Compression

Columnar data is also usually easier to compress.

Consider:

Chennai
Chennai
Chennai
Bangalore
Chennai
Chennai

Values in the same column often have similar characteristics.

Compare that with row-oriented data where different data types are mixed together:

101, John, Chennai, 25, 50000
102, Priya, Bangalore, 28, 65000

Because similar values are grouped together, columnar formats can use efficient encoding and compression.

Interview point

«Columnar storage improves analytical performance not only through column pruning, but also through better compression and efficient processing.»

---

6. Vectorized Processing

Columnar data also works well with vectorized execution.

Instead of processing values one by one:

value → process
value → process
value → process

The engine can process batches of values together.

Think:

«Instead of checking 1 person at a time, check a whole group at once.»

This improves CPU efficiency for analytical workloads.

---

7. Row vs Columnar — Interview Comparison

Feature| Row Storage| Columnar Storage
Data organization| By row| By column
Best for| OLTP| OLAP / Analytics
Full record lookup| Excellent| Less ideal
Selecting few columns| Less efficient| Very efficient
Compression| Good| Usually better for analytics
Analytical scans| Less efficient| Very efficient
Inserts/updates| Good| Generally less suited
Example| Traditional relational tables| Parquet, ORC

Memory Trick

«OLTP → Rows
OLAP → Columns»

Not an absolute rule, but an excellent interview mental model.

---

8. Simple Analogy

Imagine a supermarket.

Row-oriented

You store everything about each customer in one box:

Customer 1 → Name + Age + City + Salary
Customer 2 → Name + Age + City + Salary
Customer 3 → Name + Age + City + Salary

Column-oriented

You have separate boxes:

Name Box
Age Box
City Box
Salary Box

If the manager only asks:

«"Give me everyone's salary."»

You only open the Salary Box.

That's the fundamental advantage of columnar storage.

---

9. Important Interview Question

Q: Why is columnar storage faster for analytical queries?

Answer:

«Columnar storage is faster for analytical queries because analytical queries often access only a subset of columns from a large dataset. Columnar formats allow the engine to read only the required columns, reducing I/O and data transfer. They also provide better compression and enable efficient vectorized processing.»

---

10. When Would Row Storage Be Better?

Don't say:

«"Columnar is always better."»

That's incorrect.

Row storage is often better when:

- Applications retrieve complete records
- There are frequent point lookups
- There are frequent inserts/updates
- Workload is transactional

Example:

SELECT *
FROM orders
WHERE order_id = 1001;

If the application needs the entire order, row-oriented storage can be a natural fit.

