19. The Most Important Parquet Interview Questions

Q1. What is Parquet?

«Parquet is a columnar storage file format designed for analytical workloads. It supports efficient column access, compression, encoding and metadata-based data skipping.»

---

Q2. Why is Parquet faster than CSV for analytics?

«Because Parquet stores data column-wise, allowing query engines to read only the required columns. It also supports efficient compression, encoding and metadata-based data skipping.»

---

Q3. What is column pruning?

«Reading only the columns required by a query instead of scanning every column.»

---

Q4. What is predicate pushdown?

«Applying filter conditions as early as possible so that unnecessary data is eliminated before it travels through the rest of the query execution process.»

---

Q5. What is data skipping?

«Using metadata/statistics to determine that certain data blocks cannot satisfy a query and therefore skipping them.»

---

Q6. What is a row group?

«A row group is a horizontal group of rows within a Parquet file. Each row group contains column chunks for the columns in that group.»

---

Q7. What is a column chunk?

«A column chunk contains the data for a particular column within a particular row group.»

---

Q8. What is a page?

«A page is a smaller unit inside a Parquet column chunk used to organize and store column data.»

---

Q9. What is the Parquet hierarchy?

«File → Row Group → Column Chunk → Page.»

---

Q10. What is the difference between encoding and compression?

«Encoding changes how values are represented efficiently, while compression reduces the size of the encoded data.»

---

Q11. Why does Parquet compress well?

«Because values from the same column are stored together, and similar/repeated values can be encoded and compressed efficiently.»

---

Q12. When would row storage be better than columnar storage?

«For transactional workloads where applications frequently retrieve complete records or perform frequent inserts and updates.»

---

